  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 525 PM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper-level ridging will continue through the rest of today\nbringing mild temperatures to the region. Over the next couple \ndays, an upper-level low will track south along the Pacific coast \nand briefly settle next to Central California. This influx of \nsouthwesterly flow will start to bring precipitation chances to \nSouthwest Montana as early as Thursday morning ahead of an upper-\nlevel trough arriving.\n\nThursday into Friday, the upper-level trough comes through and \nbrings more widespread chances for precipitation along with cooler\ntemperatures that are expected to last through the weekend. With \nsnow levels dropping as low as 5000ft initially, snow is expected \nacross much of the mountains in Central and Southwest Montana. At \nlower elevations below 5000ft, rain is expected to be the dominant\nprecipitation type as much of the precipitation is expected to \nmove through the area before snow levels would otherwise drop low \nenough to produce lower elevation snow. That being said, \nsignificant uncertainty remains in how low snow levels will get \nand even slight adjustments could have significant impacts on the \nforecast.\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nLower Elevation Snow Potential: The highest uncertainty revolves \naround how quickly snow levels will drop and whether that will \ncoincide with the precipitation. Currently snow levels are \nexpected to drop below 4000ft on Sunday morning which is after the\nmajority of the precipitation has exited the area. While there \nmight be some snowflakes possible Sunday morning, it would not be \nparticularly impactful given the current setup. Should the cold \nair arrive earlier or the precipitation continue longer depending \non how fast the trough moves, these scenarios could impact how \nmuch snow is possible at the lower elevations. Right now the \nprobability of locations below 4000ft seeing at least an inch of \nsnow is about 10-20%.   -thor",
